// case: list-read-into-the-wrong-annotation
// what: reading an element out of a list hands back the list's element type, so
//      a binding annotated as something else has to be rejected. A read used to
//      answer "unknown", and an unknown is compatible with every annotation, so
//      the mismatch had nowhere to be caught -- and the annotation went on to
//      the emitter, which declared the destination register from it. The int
//      came back out of the accessor in an int register, the annotation said
//      string, and print dereferenced the number:
//
//        $ orbit check tests/negative/n49_list_read_element_type.orb   (before)
//        Checked ...: no errors.
//        $ orbit run tests/negative/n49_list_read_element_type.orb     (before)
//        Segmentation fault (core dumped)
//
//      Nothing is pinned on the crash: a segfault moves per run and per machine.
//      The class is the checkable part, and the front end now answers it.
// expect-error: 'v' is declared string but the value is int
fn f(xs: list<int>) -> int {
    val v: string = xs.get(0)
    return v.len()
}

fn main() -> int {
    return f([1, 2, 3])
}